    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>路線</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Pacaymayo</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Inca Trail to Machu Picchu                                                   <br>             <br>         Pacaymayo Campsite:         <br> This is the second day campsite. You will probably arrive early and will be able to have a well deserved rest. Here you will find cold showers and stand up bathrooms with wash basins.              <br>         Visit our website  (http://www.peruviansoul.com)         <br>         Visit us on facebook  (http://www.facebook.com/pages/Peruvian-Soul/377246490362) <br>         Visit our Inca Trail Photo Gallery! (http://www.peruviansoul.com/PhotoGallery/En/AventuraIncaTrail.aspx) 	  	       	                                  <br>                                                      <br>                                                                             <br>             Peruvian Soul 2011]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-503-DB4436-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            -72.498116,-13.233759,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Wiñaywayna</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Inca Trail to Machu Picchu                                                   <br>             <br>         Wiñaywayna Campsite:         <br> This is the third and last campsite of the Inca Trail. This campsite has some facilities. You will find bathrooms, hot showers, electricity and food including cold beers. However keep in mind that there will probably be lot of people trying to use the facilities so you will have form a que and be patient until it is your turn. For electricity and hot showers they will charge you S/.5 which is a bit less than $2.              <br>         Visit our website  (http://www.peruviansoul.com)         <br>         Visit us on facebook  (http://www.facebook.com/pages/Peruvian-Soul/377246490362) <br>         Visit our Inca Trail Photo Gallery! (http://www.peruviansoul.com/PhotoGallery/En/AventuraIncaTrail.aspx) 	  	       	                                  <br>                                                      <br>                                                                             <br>             Peruvian Soul 2011]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-503-DB4436-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            -72.537666,-13.189857,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Huayllabamba</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Inca Trail to Machu Picchu                                                   <br>             <br>         Huayllabamba Campsite:         <br> Huayllabamba is a small Andean village where the community, in agreement with the Peruvian government provides their terraces to tour operators in order to use them as campsites. Usually most of the sites have bathrooms but do not expect there to be one like at home because they are just stand up bathrooms with wash basins. Huayllabamba campsite is a nice spot but be prepared to be awoken by roosters very early in the morning. Huayllabamba is also the last town on route where you will be able to find some shops offering snacks and bottled water.              <br>         Visit our website  (http://www.peruviansoul.com)         <br>         Visit us on facebook  (http://www.facebook.com/pages/Peruvian-Soul/377246490362) <br>         Visit our Inca Trail Photo Gallery! (http://www.peruviansoul.com/PhotoGallery/En/AventuraIncaTrail.aspx) 	  	       	                                  <br>                                                      <br>                                                                             <br>             Peruvian Soul 2011]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-503-DB4436-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            -72.447091,-13.264601,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Piscacucho</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Inca Trail to Machu Picchu                                                   <br>             <br>         Piscacucho Checkpoint:         <br> Piscacucho is the first checkpoint for all trekkers starting the Inca Trail. SERNANP (Servicio Nacional de Áreas Naturales Protegidas por el Estado) is the government entity in charge of the National Park System. The function of this control is to make sure that all the groups have their documentation in order and the appropriate equipment to guarantee a safe journey. First aid kits will be checked and bags of the porters will be weight so they don’t exceed the weight limit restriction. Agents will also verify you came with an authorized tourism agency and that you have your permits. Don’t forget to carry your passport with you, otherwise you won’t be allowed to pass. In this first checkpoint at Piscacucho you can ask the agents to stamp your passport for proof of your adventure! After passing this control you are officially on the trail!               <br>         Visit our website  (http://www.peruviansoul.com)         <br>         Visit us on facebook<br>    Visit our Inca Trail Photo Gallery! (http://www.peruviansoul.com/PhotoGallery/En/AventuraIncaTrail.aspx)   (http://www.facebook.com/pages/Peruvian-Soul/377246490362) 	  	       	                                  <br>                                                      <br>                                                                             <br>             Peruvian Soul 2011]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-503-DB4436-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            -72.383778,-13.215858,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Huayllabamba</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Inca Trail to Machu Picchu                                                   <br>             <br>         Huayllabamba:         <br> This is the second checkpoint on route where agents will verify that porters, guides, and passengers have their documentation in order.                <br>         Visit our website  (http://www.peruviansoul.com)         <br>         Visit us on facebook<br>    Visit our Inca Trail Photo Gallery! (http://www.peruviansoul.com/PhotoGallery/En/AventuraIncaTrail.aspx)  (http://www.facebook.com/pages/Peruvian-Soul/377246490362) 	  	       	                                  <br>                                                      <br>                                                                             <br>             Peruvian Soul 2011]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-503-DB4436-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            -72.44979,-13.264128,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Wiñaywayna</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Inca Trail to Machu Picchu                                                   <br>             <br>         Wiñaywayna:         <br> This checkpoint is opened at 5:30am but groups usually start a line here between 3:00 and 4:00am in the morning in order to be the first groups to start the final path that leads to the famous citadel of Machu Picchu.                <br>         Visit our website  (http://www.peruviansoul.com)         <br>         Visit us on facebook<br>    Visit our Inca Trail Photo Gallery! (http://www.peruviansoul.com/PhotoGallery/En/AventuraIncaTrail.aspx)  (http://www.facebook.com/pages/Peruvian-Soul/377246490362) 	  	       	                                  <br>                                                      <br>                                                                             <br>             Peruvian Soul 2011]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-503-DB4436-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            -72.537735,-13.189457,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Machu Picchu</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Inca Trail to Machu Picchu                                                   <br>             <br>         Machu Picchu:         <br> When you reach Machu Picchu you will first have to head down to the official checkpoint and exit the site. This is done for several reasons; in order to help the agents to control the number of people entering the citadel and for general security. Also, keep in mind that walking sticks are forbidden in the site, so be sure to put them in your bag before heading back to the entrance. Big backpacks are also forbidden, so if you decided to carry yours you will have to leave it at the luggage storages.  There are no bathrooms in the park, take your time and use toilets at this point. You will also find shops and restaurants. After passing this last control you will be able to visit Machu Picchu.              <br>         Visit our website  (http://www.peruviansoul.com)         <br>         Visit us on facebook<br>    Visit our Inca Trail Photo Gallery! (http://www.peruviansoul.com/PhotoGallery/En/AventuraIncaTrail.aspx)  (http://www.facebook.com/pages/Peruvian-Soul/377246490362) 	  	       	                                  <br>                                                      <br>                                                                             <br>             Peruvian Soul 2011]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-503-DB4436-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            -72.543603,-13.166424,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Inca Trail</name>
